{"gid":"2026_03_ARI_SF","week":3,"away":"ARI","home":"SF","away_col":"#E64868","home_col":"#E8506A","spread":11.5,"total":46.5,"fav":"SF","hpts":29,"apts":18,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 27 \u00b7 16:05","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> SF are run-first (40% pass when leading) into a ARI front that bleeds <strong>128 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.47 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>SF</strong> host the <strong>ARI</strong> as favorites of <strong>11.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>46.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>SF 29\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>56% of its snaps with SF in front</strong>, <strong>26% within one score</strong>, and <strong>18% with SF chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>SF should control</strong>. A favorite of 12 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>SF</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>ARI</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>ARI</strong> to run more total plays than SF \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for ARI's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Efficiency edge to SF \u2014 a 47% success-rate offense against a ARI defense that allows 48% \u2014 points should come.</p><p><strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 ARI give up 23 rec yds/game to backs, and SF target the RB on 25% of throws.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 SF hold WR1s to just 51 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>George Kittle</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 ARI are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Trey McBride</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 SF are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Mike Evans</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ARI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone ARI throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans SF.</strong> As a 12-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ARI throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>.</p><p>Fade the ARI run \u2014 as 12-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>Tyler Allgeier</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>SF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>SF are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 12-point favorite projected to lead for 56% of snaps, <strong>SF</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>19 carries for 87 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as SF salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Mike Evans</strong> (10 targets, 91 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when SF lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>George Kittle</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 88 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ARI \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>ARI are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ARI project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tyler Allgeier</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 65 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ARI funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 81 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Trey McBride</strong> (TE, 13 targets, 98 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 12-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"ARI","col":"#E64868","players":[{"name":"Jacoby Brissett","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":257.31,"book":null,"over_th":245.36,"under_th":271.69,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.42,"book":null,"over_th":1.0,"under_th":1.39,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":10.4,"fair":"9/1","book":null,"value":null},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":257.31,"q":[119.48,191.63,258.79,323.29,388.32],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"pass_td":{"m":1.42,"q":[0.22,0.22,1.19,2.16,3.13],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_yards":{"m":14.43,"q":[0.0,2.2,11.82,22.4,32.99],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_att":{"m":3.14,"q":[0.0,0.81,3.14,4.3,6.63],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null}}},{"name":"Jeremiyah Love","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":64.12,"book":null,"over_th":49.5,"under_th":61.88,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Carries","proj":15.68,"book":null,"over_th":12.83,"under_th":15.27,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Rec yards",